.TH ws_extend 1 "April 2026" "USER COMMANDS"

.SH NAME
ws_extend \- extend the lifetime of a workspace

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ws_extend
[\-F FILESYSTEM] WORKSPACE DAYS

.SH DESCRIPTION
Extend an existing
.B workspace
to last until DAYS days from today.
This is a convenience wrapper around
.B ws_allocate \-x

Each extension consumes one of the allowed extensions for the workspace,
unless the new expiration date is shorter than the previous one.
The number of available extensions can be seen with
.B ws_list
and is limited by the administrator.

The administrator can require that a part of the current lifetime of the
workspace has elapsed before it can be extended (for example 75%, so a
60 day workspace can be extended from day 45 on). An earlier request fails,
prints the date from which the workspace can be extended, and does not
consume an extension. A request for more days than allowed fails as well
and does not consume an extension.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-F FILESYSTEM
select the filesystem the workspace resides in.
See
.B ws_list
\-l
for a list of available filesystems.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
extend a workspace by 10 more days from today:
.B ws_extend myworkspace 10

.TP
extend a workspace on a specific filesystem:
.B ws_extend -F scratch myworkspace 10


.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ws (7),
.BR ws_release (1),
.BR ws_list (1),
.BR ws_find, (1),
.BR ws_restore (1),
.BR ws_extend (1),
.BR ws_send_ical (1)

